WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.092
The sigmoid function is an S-shaped curve.

00:00:03.092 --> 00:00:06.892
Its input, z, can be any real number.

00:00:06.892 --> 00:00:10.782
Its output always stays between zero and one.

00:00:10.782 --> 00:00:14.817
When z equals zero, the probability is one half.

00:00:14.817 --> 00:00:18.892
Large positive scores move the probability toward one.

00:00:18.892 --> 00:00:21.740
Large negative scores move it toward zero.

00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:24.382
Run the code and inspect the curve.

00:00:24.382 --> 00:00:29.067
Notice that the probability changes most rapidly around the center.

00:00:29.067 --> 00:00:36.012
The important connection is this: logistic regression still creates a linear score from the features,

00:00:36.012 --> 00:00:42.877
but instead of treating that score as the final prediction, it transforms the score into a probability.

00:00:42.877 --> 00:00:50.133
This visualization helps explain what the model is doing mathematically without requiring advanced calculus.
